WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.040
There are lots of places in the&nbsp;
world which have the same name twice.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:04.400
Places like the Sahara Desert:

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.400
well, Sahara comes from the Arabic for "desert",

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.160
so that's the Desert Desert.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.480
Or the River Avon. Avon originally meant "River",

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.160
so it's the River River.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.360
There are a few triple names&nbsp;
like that in the world, but this,

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.800
Torpenhow Hill in the Lake&nbsp;
District, in north-west of England,

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:22.640
is the only place in the world with&nbsp;
four words that all mean the same thing.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:23.920
Tor, Old English for hill.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:25.360
Penn, Old Welsh for hill,

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:27.840
How, Old English for a slightly&nbsp;
different kind of hill,

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.360
and finally modern English, hill.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:34.400
I know it's not a massively&nbsp;
impressive place, but the name's good.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.560
Except that's a story that's&nbsp;been
debunked quite a few times.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.720
Double and triple place names&nbsp;
are a thing, that's true.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.520
They tend to happen because&nbsp;
centuries ago locals would name

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:46.880
the nearby hill, or river, or&nbsp;
mountain, or desert, the obvious name,

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:48.080
like, "the hill",

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:49.920
and then when folks with other languages arrived,

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.000
for trade or conquest or exploration,

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.440
they added their word for it as well.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.280
But the trouble here is: there is no&nbsp;
Torpenhow Hill marked on any official map.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.360
The name's almost certainly a modern invention.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:04.320
Someone noticed the village of&nbsp;
Torpenhow about a mile away,

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.240
and spotted the three words for hill there,

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.280
and decided to add the modern&nbsp;
English "hill" to it as well.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.280
And: tor, penn, and how all have more&nbsp;
complicated definitions than "hill".

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.840
It's not really "hill hill hill", that village,

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:20.160
you can plausibly translate "torpenhow"&nbsp;
as, well, the land the village sits on:

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.880
a small rise jutting out&nbsp;
from a much bigger incline.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.400
This is just a bump on the&nbsp;
side of an actual big hill.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.800
I don't have a drone with me, but&nbsp;
Google Earth shows it well enough.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:33.600
Also... the village's name is&nbsp;
"Trepenna". It's spelled "Torpenhow",

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.840
but the local pronunciation is "trepenna".

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.640
So you can tell who's done the research when&nbsp;
this is mentioned in a list of weird place names.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:43.280
In reality, there's just&nbsp;
the village of "Trepenna".

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:46.720
Tor-pen-how Hill doesn't exist. That's the debunk.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:49.760
And so now, I'm going to debunk the debunk.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:53.840
Because if that's true, why am I out&nbsp;
of breath? What did I just climb?

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:59.120
I came up here from the village, it's&nbsp;
a slow steady climb on a country road.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.600
The land around this point is lower, and it&nbsp;
sits right next to Trepenna-slash-Torpenhow.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:08.160
Now, I'm not going to colonise the Lake District&nbsp;
and tell them how they should be naming things.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.480
But there have been plenty of tourist&nbsp;
attractions built around much less than this.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:16.640
A good map is like a good dictionary: it&nbsp;
reflects the real world, it doesn't make it.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.160
If people will go to that one tree in&nbsp;
New Zealand that looks good on Instagram,

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.240
just because they've seen other pictures of it,

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.160
they'd probably come to Hill Hill Hill Hill.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.360
People go to Four Corners in the US&nbsp;
just to be in four states at once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.720
it's not too weird to think that&nbsp;
people would want to visit here.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:35.360
Sure, the village is "Trepenna",&nbsp;
but this can be Torpenhow Hill,

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:36.560
if enough people want it to be.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:42.240
And the thing is: as I record this, if you&nbsp;
type "Torpenhow Hill" into online maps...

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:44.000
quite a few of them point you here.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.520
Actually, just over there, I'll CG in a&nbsp;
giant map marker just to make my point.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:51.360
The only thing stopping this&nbsp;
officially being "Torpenhow Hill"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.280
is that there's not enough consensus.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.520
So who decides that?

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:57.760
Because I do worry about&nbsp;
coming to places like this.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.320
This is a public footpath, I didn't&nbsp;
have to ask permission to be here,

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:04.400
but I have a duty of care to the locations&nbsp;
I visit, and to people who live there.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.360
That one tree in New Zealand is being&nbsp;
slowly destroyed by tourism and by vandals.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:13.360
Now, "Trepenna" is far enough off&nbsp;
the tourist trail so, probably,

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.760
only a few geography nerds&nbsp;
will bother to trek out here.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:21.040
But if this did start becoming popular, if&nbsp;
tourists did start arriving at Tor-pen-how Hill,

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.520
I do wonder how the locals in the&nbsp;
quiet little village would feel.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:28.400
Because the signpost that&nbsp;
says this is a public footpath

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:32.320
has mysteriously disappeared since the last&nbsp;
time the Google Street View car came by.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:33.920
I'm not saying anyone destroyed it,

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:35.920
I'm just saying no-one's bothered to replace it.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:37.280
So as for how the locals feel?

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:45.840
I probably shouldn't bother them by asking.

